  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1212 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nHeavy rain overnight\n\nAxis of showers and thunderstorms with rain rates up to 2\" per hour \nhas developed in the vicinity of an 850/700mb front as increasing \nmoisture transport interacted with this front and around 1000 J/kg \nof elevated instability helped generate stronger updrafts compared \nwith the showers earlier Friday. Observations of around 2-5\" have \nbeen seen in northern Fillmore and northern Mower Counties under \nthis band. Additionally, an area of scattered showers and storms is \nseen around Mason City ahead of a disturbance located over north \ncentral IA as of 03z. If current observational trends hold, areas \nfrom Austin MN to La Crosse to Richland Center will see the heavy \namounts, potentially around 6\".\n\nAdditional heavy rain possible Saturday afternoon\n\nAs a shortwave currently over western Nebraska ejects eastward and \nlower level moist advection continues, short window with far \nsouthwest Wisconsin and adjacent parts of northeast Iowa to be south \nof the surface front. Thus, as the Nebraska shortwave approaches, \nadditional showers and thunderstorms will likely (60-80%) develop \nthere. With PWATs still near 2\", efficient rain rates are once again \nfavored. Of particular concern is the potential for this round of \nrain to overlap with where heavy rain occurs overnight Friday night. \nShould this occur, flooding concerns would be heightened.\nFinally, while highly unlikely given the tendency for\ntemperature profiles to be moist adiabatic, the window with\nsurface-based thunderstorms introduces a risk (1%) for a stray \ntornado as sfc-500m helicity values look to reach close to 100\nm2/s2.",
